"""Original monitoring, defensive analysis and response scenarios."""
ITEMS=[
('A SIEM receives every raw authentication event, but the normalized user field is empty after a vendor changes its log format. Which pipeline correction addresses the lost context?',
 ['Update the parser rules for the changed layout','Restart the forwarding agent on the source host','Raise the SIEM ingestion license limit','Increase retention for the raw authentication data'],
 'Raw events still arrive, so collection works; the parser no longer extracts the user field from the new format. Restarting the agent, more license or longer retention leaves parsing broken.'),
('A dashboard counts every transaction twice. Both copies share the same source event identifier but arrive through separate collectors. Which change targets the inflation?',
 ['Deduplicate on the source event ID','Raise the alert threshold to double its value','Rewrite the parser and field mappings for the transaction source','Switch the dashboard to a weekly average'],
 'The same event arrives through two collectors, so one path must go or the pipeline must deduplicate on the event ID. Doubling thresholds or averaging hides the duplicates instead of fixing them.'),
('One application has produced no central events since its forwarding agent stopped. Other sources and parsing remain healthy. What should be restored first?',
 ['The agent that ships its logs','The parser rules for the application logs','The correlation rules that use those logs','The dashboard panels for that application'],
 'No events arrive because the agent stopped, so collection must be restored first. Parsers and rules cannot work on events that never reach the SIEM.'),
('Events from two systems are consistently eight hours apart even though raw timestamps describe the same activity. One parser treats local time as UTC. Which correction is needed?',
 ['Configure that parser with the source time zone','Deduplicate the events from the two systems','Restart NTP on both source systems','Shift the correlation window to nine hours'],
 'The parser reads local time as UTC, so it must apply the correct time zone. The source clocks are fine, and widening the correlation window hides the error.'),
('Two equally credible compromise alerts affect otherwise similar hosts. One supports emergency clinical care and the other a disposable training environment. Which factor should most directly distinguish response priority?',
 ['The business criticality of each host','The CVSS score of the exploited flaw','The time each alert was received','The vendor severity label on each alert'],
 'With equal technical evidence, the host that supports emergency care has far greater consequences, so criticality sets priority. CVSS and vendor labels are the same here, and arrival order is not risk.'),
('A feed labels an indicator severe but provides no provenance, context or corroboration. Before treating that label as reliable, which characteristic should an analyst assess?',
 ['How trustworthy its source has been','The number of other feeds that ingest it','The age of the indicator in days','The format the feed uses to deliver it'],
 'An indicator is only as useful as its source and evidence, so the analyst assesses reliability and confidence. Wide redistribution, age or format do not show it is accurate.'),
('A backup server transfers large volumes nightly as expected. A rule flags its ordinary schedule as an anomaly. What context should be used to evaluate the alert?',
 ['Its usual schedule for that time of night','A single volume threshold for every host','The geographic location of the destination','The patch level of the backup server'],
 'Comparing with the server\'s own baseline shows the nightly volume is normal for its role. A single threshold for every host mislabels high-volume roles, and patch level says nothing about the transfer.'),
('A single login anomaly is inconclusive. The analyst can obtain endpoint and application events for the same account and interval. Which method improves confidence in the incident hypothesis?',
 ['Correlate with other data for that account','Rerun the same login rule with a lower threshold','Raise the alert severity to high','Wait for the anomaly to repeat tomorrow'],
 'Independent endpoint and application evidence can confirm or refute the login anomaly. Rerunning the same rule repeats one source, and raising severity adds no evidence.'),
('A report service constructs SQL by combining fixed query structure with untrusted filter values. Which change directly prevents those values from becoming query syntax?',
 ['Use parameterized queries with bound values','HTML-encode the filter values before use','Add an object-level authorization check','Restrict outbound database connections'],
 'Bound parameters keep values as data so they cannot change the SQL. HTML encoding protects browsers, not the database, and authorization does not separate data from syntax.'),
('A dashboard inserts an untrusted description into an HTML text context. The description must appear as text, not executable markup. Which defense addresses this output boundary?',
 ['Encoding the value before it reaches the page','Parameterized queries when storing the description','An allowlist of outbound server destinations','Server-side validation of the description length'],
 'Encoding for the HTML context makes the browser show the value as text. Parameterized queries protect SQL, and length checks do not stop markup.'),
('A server-side preview feature only needs to fetch from two approved content services, but currently accepts arbitrary destinations. Which restriction targets the unnecessary network reach?',
 ['Allowlist the two services and block internal ranges','HTML-encode the fetched preview content','Bind the URL as a query parameter','Require users to sign in with MFA before using the preview'],
 'Allowing only the two services and blocking internal ranges prevents server-side request forgery. Encoding and parameter binding do not limit where the server connects, and login still lets users choose targets.'),
('An API authenticates callers and safely parses record identifiers, but returns any customer\'s record when its identifier is supplied. Which missing check addresses this access flaw?',
 ['Check that this customer belongs to the requester','Validate that the identifier is a well-formed UUID','Rate-limit requests to the record endpoint','Encode the record fields in the response'],
 'This is broken object-level authorization: the API must check that the caller may access the specific record. Format checks and rate limits do not stop access to another customer\'s data.'),
('Two concurrent redemption requests each read a voucher as unused before either marks it used, so both succeed. Which fix addresses the race at its source?',
 ['An atomic compare-and-set on the voucher status','Validation of the voucher code format','A rate limit on redemption requests per user','Logging of every redemption request'],
 'Doing the check and the update as one atomic operation stops both requests from consuming the voucher. Format checks, rate limits and logging leave the race window open.'),
('A production service includes an unused diagnostic endpoint with privileged capabilities. No business flow requires it. Which remediation most directly eliminates that attack surface?',
 ['Remove the endpoint from production','Put the endpoint behind the WAF','Add alerting on calls to the endpoint','Require an API key to call the endpoint'],
 'Removing an unneeded privileged endpoint eliminates the exposure. A WAF, alerts or an API key leave the capability reachable.'),
('A vulnerable library has reached end of support and has no patched release. The application still needs its function, and a maintained alternative has been validated. Which durable remediation fits?',
 ['Move to the replacement package','Add a WAF rule blocking the known exploit','Pin the vulnerable version in the lock file','Accept the risk for another year'],
 'Moving to a maintained library restores ongoing security fixes. A WAF rule covers only known exploits, and pinning or accepting keeps the unsupported code.'),
('A live application credential was committed to a public repository. The current file has been cleaned, but the credential remains valid. Which action addresses continued use of the leaked value?',
 ['Revoke and replace the value','Rewrite the repository history to purge it','Make the repository private','Add a secret scanner to the pipeline'],
 'Anyone who copied the value can still use it, so it must be revoked and replaced. Purging history or making the repository private does not invalidate copies, and scanning prevents future leaks only.'),
('An investigator receives original media needed for a formal inquiry. Before ordinary analysis modifies files, which evidence-handling priority applies?',
 ['Acquire a hashed forensic image with a write blocker','Mount the original media read-write to triage it','Run antivirus on the original media first','Copy the files of interest to a USB drive'],
 'The original must be preserved, so the investigator images it through a write blocker and records the hash. Mounting it read-write, scanning it or copying selected files can change evidence.'),
('A forensic image has been acquired and its integrity verified. The analyst needs to perform repeated searches without altering the preserved master. Which artifact should be used?',
 ['A verified working copy of the image','The original seized drive','The master image mounted read-write','A logical export of selected folders'],
 'Analysis on a verified working copy leaves the master untouched. Working on the original or a read-write master risks changes, and a partial export loses context.'),
('An evidence package moves from an incident responder to an external examiner. Which record supports accountability for that handoff?',
 ['A chain of custody form signed by both parties','A new SHA-256 hash of the image','A ticket noting the package was shipped','An email confirming the examiner received it'],
 'A chain of custody entry records who released and received the evidence, when and why. A hash proves the data is unchanged but not who handled it.'),
('An investigation combines UTC server logs, local endpoint times and a device with a known clock offset. Which timeline practice preserves interpretability?',
 ['Convert to UTC and keep the originals','Replace every timestamp with the converted UTC value','Use local time for all sources in the timeline','Drop events from the device with the clock offset'],
 'Normalizing to UTC while keeping originals and documenting offsets lets others check the timeline. Overwriting originals loses provenance, and dropping the offset device loses evidence.'),
('The incident lead needs evidence of a suspicious process\'s current in-memory state before an approved shutdown. Which acquisition target is most time-sensitive?',
 ['A RAM capture of the running host','A full image of the system disk','The cloud control-plane audit logs','The firewall flow records for the host'],
 'Memory is lost at shutdown, so it must be captured first. The disk, cloud logs and flow records persist after power-off.'),
('Investigators need deleted-file remnants and filesystem metadata from a powered-off workstation. Which evidence source most directly addresses that question?',
 ['A full image of the disk','A memory capture from the workstation','The cloud provider audit trail','The proxy logs for the workstation'],
 'Deleted-file remnants and filesystem metadata live on the disk, captured by a full image. A powered-off machine has no memory to capture.'),
('A storage policy was changed through a provider\'s management API. Investigators need the actor and request history for that control-plane action. Which source is most relevant?',
 ['The cloud audit trail, such as CloudTrail','A disk image of the application server','Memory from the administrator laptop','Packet captures from the office network'],
 'Management API calls are recorded in the provider\'s audit log with actor and request details. Host images and office captures may not contain provider-side events.'),
('The team needs to determine which observed internal host contacted a destination during a specific interval. Packet or flow telemetry was collected at the relevant boundary. Which source should be preserved?',
 ['The saved NetFlow and PCAP data','The current firewall rule set','The endpoint antivirus quarantine list','The DHCP scope configuration'],
 'Flow or packet records directly show which host contacted the destination. Current rules and configurations show how things are set now, not what happened.'),
('A hunt begins with a broad concern about misuse of administrative tools but no defined behavior or scope to investigate. What should the team establish first?',
 ['A specific hypothesis tied to an ATT&CK technique','A full export of every log source available','A new SIEM alert for administrative tools','A list of all administrators in the directory'],
 'A testable hypothesis about a specific behavior focuses the hunt. Exporting everything or writing alerts before knowing what to look for wastes effort.'),
('A hunt hypothesis concerns unusual parent-child process relationships, but only firewall events are available. What must the team identify next?',
 ['Endpoint process creation telemetry','Additional firewall rules for the hosts','Threat intelligence feeds listing hashes of the suspected tools','Packet captures of internet traffic'],
 'Parent-child relationships come from endpoint process telemetry. Firewall, threat feed and packet data cannot show which process started which.'),
('The team has gathered the necessary records for a defined hypothesis. Some observations support it and others contradict it. What analytical step should follow?',
 ['Weigh both supporting and contradicting evidence','Report the supporting evidence as confirmed','Discard the observations that contradict it','Restart the hunt with a broader scope'],
 'Every relevant observation must be weighed, including the ones that contradict the hypothesis. Keeping only supporting evidence is confirmation bias.'),
('A completed hunt identifies a meaningful behavior that existing alerts miss. After validating its relevance, what operational improvement should follow?',
 ['Turn the finding into a tested detection rule','Repeat the same manual hunt every quarter','Record the finding only in the hunt report','Lower the severity of the related alerts'],
 'A validated finding should become continuous coverage through a tested detection. Repeating it manually or only filing it leaves the gap open between hunts.'),
('An analyst wants a reusable rule describing characteristic byte or text patterns in suspicious files. Which artifact type most directly serves that purpose?',
 ['A YARA rule','A Sigma rule','A STIX indicator','A TAXII collection'],
 'YARA rules match byte and string patterns in files. Sigma targets log events, STIX describes threat information, and TAXII transports it.'),
('A team wants to express a detection over normalized log events in a portable rule format before translating it to its SIEM. Which artifact type fits?',
 ['A Sigma rule','A YARA rule','A Snort signature','An OpenIOC file'],
 'Sigma is a portable format for log-based detections that converts to SIEM queries. YARA matches file content, and Snort matches network packets.'),
('Several organizations need a machine-readable representation of an indicator and its relationships to observed activity. Which artifact category fits?',
 ['Linked STIX domain objects','A TAXII server endpoint','A YARA rule set','A CSV list of IP addresses and domain names'],
 'STIX represents indicators, threat actors and their relationships in structured form. TAXII moves STIX data, and a CSV list cannot express relationships.'),
('Partners already agree on the structured threat data format and now need an API mechanism to exchange collections. Which capability addresses delivery?',
 ['A TAXII server','A STIX bundle schema','A Sigma rule repository','A YARA rule feed'],
 'TAXII is the API for exchanging threat intelligence collections. STIX defines the data format the partners already agreed on.'),
('An incident is actively spreading between approved workload zones. The response lead authorizes a narrow temporary boundary that stops propagation while preserving essential operations. Which response purpose does it serve?',
 ['Containment to limit further harm','Eradication of the attacker foothold','Recovery of the affected services','Lessons learned after closure'],
 'A temporary boundary that stops spread is containment. It does not remove the cause or restore services.'),
('The affected host is isolated, and analysts have identified an unauthorized startup component that would survive ordinary process termination. Which response objective targets that component?',
 ['Eradication of the persistence from the host','Containment by keeping the host isolated','Recovery of the host from backup','Detection tuning for the startup event'],
 'Removing the persistence mechanism is eradication. Isolation only contains it, and restoring a backup might bring it back if it predates the backup.'),
('The cause has been addressed and the approved recovery copy has been checked. The team now returns the service to controlled operation and monitors it. Which phase objective is being carried out?',
 ['Restoring operations under watch','Containment of the original threat','Eradication of remaining artifacts','Preparation for the next incident'],
 'Returning a validated service to operation under watch is recovery. Containment and eradication are already complete.'),
('The immediate incident is resolved, but the team needs to understand how an unauthorized trust change passed the change process. Which activity supports prevention of recurrence?',
 ['A root cause analysis','Closing the ticket with a summary note','Restoring the trust setting from backup','Adding the account to a watch list'],
 'Root cause analysis looks at why the process let the change through so it can be fixed. Closing the ticket or reverting the setting does not explain or prevent recurrence.'),
('An approved investigation requires records from the affected period, and the ordinary lifecycle job would delete them tomorrow. Counsel and the incident lead have authorized preservation. What should the log owner do?',
 ['Place the needed records on hold','Let the job run and rely on backups','Export the records to a shared drive before the job runs','Shorten the retention for other logs'],
 'An authorized hold must stop the deletion of the needed records. Relying on backups risks loss, and copying to a shared drive breaks access control and custody.'),
('Application logs record full authentication secrets even though troubleshooting only requires a request identifier and outcome. Which logging change reduces unnecessary exposure?',
 ['Redact them at the source','Encrypt the log files at rest','Extend log retention to support audits','Restrict the logs to the SIEM team'],
 'Secrets should never be logged, so they must be removed or masked at the source. Encryption and access limits still keep the secrets stored.'),
('A compromised application account can modify both its live data and every copy of its audit trail. Which logging architecture weakness should be addressed?',
 ['Ship records to write-once storage it cannot touch','Add more fields and a sequence number to each audit record','Increase the audit log verbosity','Rotate the audit files more often'],
 'Audit logs must sit outside the reach of the account they record, for example in immutable storage. More fields, verbosity or rotation do not stop alteration.'),
('Policy requires older records to remain retrievable, but the team has never attempted a restore from the archive tier. Which check demonstrates usable retention?',
 ['Restore and read a sample of archived records','Confirm the archive storage invoice is paid','Check the archive retention setting in the console','Count the objects in the archive tier'],
 'Only a real restore shows that archived records can be retrieved and read. Billing, settings and object counts do not prove recoverability.'),
('A detection validation set contains 200 known malicious events. The rule misses 50 of them. Using false negatives divided by all actual malicious events, what is the false-negative rate?',
 ['25 percent','20 percent','33 percent','75 percent'],
 '50 divided by 200 is 0.25, or 25%. Dividing by the 150 detected events gives 33%, and 75% is the detection rate instead.'),
('Analysts review 150 alerts and confirm 60 are genuine incidents. Using true positives divided by all positive alerts, what is the alert precision?',
 ['40 percent','60 percent','67 percent','90 percent'],
 '60 divided by 150 is 0.40, or 40%. The 90 false alerts make up the other 60%.'),
('A test includes 80 malicious events. A detector correctly alerts on 48. Using detected malicious events divided by all malicious events, what is its recall?',
 ['60 percent','40 percent','48 percent','80 percent'],
 '48 divided by 80 is 0.60, or 60%. The 32 missed events are the other 40%.'),
('A control assessment finds 90 of 120 required high-priority sources sending usable events. What percentage of those required sources has verified coverage?',
 ['75 percent','25 percent','80 percent','90 percent'],
 '90 divided by 120 is 0.75, or 75%. The 30 silent sources are the other 25%.'),
('Four confirmed incidents took 6, 10, 14 and 18 minutes from first observed activity to detection. What is the arithmetic mean detection delay?',
 ['12 minutes','18 minutes','16 minutes','10 minutes'],
 'The delays sum to 48 minutes, and 48 divided by 4 is 12 minutes. 18 minutes is only the longest delay.'),
('A response team detects an incident at 09:07 and completes authorized containment at 09:25. What is the detection-to-containment interval?',
 ['18 minutes','25 minutes','22 minutes','16 minutes'],
 'From 09:07 to 09:25 is 18 minutes. 25 is the minute shown on the clock, not the interval.'),
('A collector receives logs at 600 records per minute. A recoverable backlog contains 14,400 records, and the recovery stream processes that backlog at a net 600 records per minute after new traffic. How long will clearing it take?',
 ['24 minutes','12 minutes','48 minutes','20 minutes'],
 '14,400 divided by the net 600 records per minute is 24 minutes. The net rate already allows for new traffic, so nothing more is subtracted.'),
('A timeline source is known to run 20 minutes ahead of UTC. It records an event at 14:50 local-device time, and a trusted UTC alert is at 15:00. After correction, how long before the alert did the event occur?',
 ['30 minutes','10 minutes','20 minutes','50 minutes'],
 'The corrected event time is 14:30 UTC, 30 minutes before the alert. Using the uncorrected 14:50 gives 10 minutes.'),
('Two incidents use similar code, and a report claims they must have the same operator. Which analytical response is most appropriate before accepting that conclusion?',
 ['Seek independent corroboration beyond the shared code','Accept the claim because the code overlap is high','Assign both incidents to the most active known group','Reject the claim because code is often reused'],
 'Code can be shared, sold or copied, so attribution needs independent corroboration such as infrastructure or tradecraft. Accepting or rejecting outright skips the analysis.'),
('A service failed shortly after a security alert, but the team has no evidence connecting the two events. Which reasoning safeguard is needed?',
 ['Treat it as coincidence until a link is shown','Record the alert as the root cause of the outage in the report','Close the alert because the service recovered','Raise the alert severity because of the outage'],
 'Sequence alone does not show causation, so the link must be supported by evidence before it is reported. Recording the alert as the cause assumes what is unproven.'),
('An analyst proposes a new detection based on one unusual sample. A controlled authorized test environment is available. Which step helps assess whether the observed behavior is reproducible?',
 ['Detonate the sample in the isolated lab and compare','Deploy the detection to production immediately','Search the internet for similar samples','Ask the vendor to confirm the behavior'],
 'Running the sample again in a controlled environment shows whether the behavior repeats before a rule is built on it. Deploying immediately risks noise based on one observation.'),
('Evidence gaps prevent the team from establishing the exact first compromise time. The available records support only a bounded interval. How should the report represent the finding?',
 ['Give the bounded window and note the gaps','Report the earliest logged event as the start','Pick the midpoint of the interval as the start','Omit the start time from the report'],
 'The report should give the supported window and say where evidence is missing. Choosing the earliest log or a midpoint invents precision, and omitting it hides a key finding.'),
]
assert len(ITEMS)==52
